IDEA 1: Concept-Coverage DFF — Factor the student’s block-9 patch features \(X\) as \([X_+,(-X)_+]\approx HV\), using rank-6 nonnegative matrix factorization and 20 updates; normalize each column of \(H\) to sum to one and weight each concept by \(q_r=\sum_i a_iH_{ir}\), where \(a_i\) is final-block CLS attention.  
Starting with \(S=\varnothing\), score each remaining patch by its marginal increase in \(F(S)=\sum_r q_r\log[1+(196/K)\sum_{j\in S}H_{jr}]\); greedily retain exactly K patches, rewarding coverage of distinct student concepts with diminishing returns.  
— basis: Collins, Achanta & Süsstrunk, [*Deep Feature Factorization for Concept Discovery*, ECCV 2018](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.10206): demonstrated semantic concept and part localization from internal activations. The hypothesis is that covering multiple relevant bird parts preserves more teaching information; the signed-feature adaptation and KD benefit remain untested.  
— closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [*Beyond Attentive Tokens*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.11315) combines attention with similarity-based merging; this selects original patches through explicit concept-coverage explanations, without merging or spatial rules.  
— extra FLOPs: Approximately **0.04–0.08 GFLOPs/image** for width 192–384, counting multiply-add as two FLOPs; includes factorization and greedy selection, with no extra model forward or backward.

IDEA 2: Contrast-Evidence Sensor Selection — At the student’s penultimate block, compute \(b_{ir}=\langle X_i,\partial(z_{c_1}-z_{c_{r+1}})/\partial X_i\rangle\), \(r=1,2,3\), using its four highest-logit classes and three `autograd.grad` calls retaining the training graph; these signed attributions explain how scaling each patch state changes competing-class margins.  
Normalize \(b\) by its global RMS; initialize \(M=I_3\), repeatedly score unselected patches by \(\log(1+b_i^\top M^{-1}b_i)\), select the maximum, and update \(M\leftarrow M+b_ib_i^\top\) until K patches remain—preserving independent decision evidence using only student computation.  
— inspired by: Manohar, Brunton, Kutz & Brunton, [*Data-Driven Sparse Sensor Placement for Reconstruction*, IEEE Control Systems Magazine, 2018, arXiv:1701.07569](https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.07569).  
— why it transfers: Its strongest transferable idea is selecting expensive observations to preserve independent low-dimensional signals; demonstrated reconstruction gains on faces and fluid fields motivate treating patches as sensors of class-contrast evidence, although neither its reconstruction guarantees nor KD gains automatically transfer.  
— queries tried: interpretability neural networks spatial feature decomposition nonnegative matrix factorization semantic parts deep feature factorization; explaining classifier decisions contrastive evidence competing classes discriminative image regions; experimental design sensor selection log determinant information redundant measurements; sparse sensor placement interpretable modes pivoted QR reconstruction optimal measurements paper; neural network explanations completeness sufficient subset feature interactions concept coverage paper  
— closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [CDPruner, NeurIPS 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10967), also uses determinant-based selection; here the kernel represents signed internal margin attributions rather than instruction-weighted embedding similarity, so the proposed contribution is the explanation space, not determinant maximization.  
— extra FLOPs: **Three last-block activation-gradient passes**, roughly **0.7–3 GFLOPs/image** for width 192–384 at two FLOPs/MAC, plus under 0.001 GFLOPs for selection; no parameter gradients. Both proposals remain hypotheses requiring same-K wins against all six baselines and Full-KD-tolerance validation with selector costs included.